<p id="S1.F1.15" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.F1.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">[box, fill=graybox, draw=grayborder, line width=0.8pt, anchor=north] (hope1) at ([yshift=-5pt]vs.south-|box2) 
<span id="S1.F1.15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Ensembling</span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:4.0pt;">
Combine predictions from multiple unsupervised predictors
;</span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><span id="S1.F1.16" class="ltx_ERROR ltx_centering undefined">\node</span><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S1.F1.17" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.F1.17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">[box, fill=graybox, draw=grayborder, line width=0.8pt, anchor=north] (hope2) at ([yshift=-5pt]vs.south-|box3) 
<span id="S1.F1.17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Combining approaches</span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:4.0pt;">
Mix unsupervised and easy-to-hard generalization methods
;</span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.18" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.19" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Three challenges for the safety of unsupervised elicitation, targeted by our stress-testing evaluations. We evaluate existing methods, as well as new methods based on two hopes: ensembling multiple unsupervised predictors with the hope that at least one of them is correct, and mixing unsupervised and easy-to-hard generalization methods with the hope of getting the strength of each approach.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training of large language models (LLMs) often relies on labels created by humans for supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and human feedback for reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stiennon et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This can lead to models imitating mistakes a human would make <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or generating text containing false claims that humans would rate highly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. This problem may get worse as LLMs are applied in more complex domains where human feedback becomes increasingly unreliable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, several approaches have been developed to elicit accurate outputs from a pretrained LLM without using direct human supervision. Easy-to-hard generalization (E2H) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hase et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> involves training models on easy-to-verify tasks, with the hope that it generalizes in a way that improves performance on harder tasks. In contrast, unsupervised elicitation (UE) techniques do not use any ground-truth labels at all. For example, internal coherence maximization (ICM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> annotates a dataset without human labels by finding a set of true/false labels that are logically consistent and mutually predictable according to the LLM. Linear truth probes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark, 2024</a>)</cite> can also be trained in an unsupervised way using Contrast-Consistent Search (CCS) or PCA to find directions along which contradictory pairs of statements are separated—a method introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al. (2022)</a></cite> and investigated further by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mallen et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Though each of these approaches has been found to perform well on a variety of datasets, it is unclear whether they will perform as well when applied to real tasks which are beyond human capabilities. In this work, we consider three challenges to the UE and E2H methods, create datasets in which these challenges are present, and evaluate existing methods on these datasets. These challenges are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.1em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Salient non-truth features.</span> Datasets may contain prevalent features that are more salient to the model than truthfulness and which humans are unable to detect. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al. (2023)</a></cite> demonstrated that CCS can fail by labeling statements based on the presence of such features (e.g. whether the statement agrees with a character described in the prompt). Here, we test a wider range of UE and E2H techniques on math problems with salient features added to each prompt, and on semantic classification datasets with multiple competing salient features.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Imbalanced training sets.</span> Some UE methods require a set of training examples. However, since we do not know the ground truth labels of these examples, the distribution of labels might be imbalanced. For example, when eliciting models to identify malicious code, it is likely that most of the examples available will be benign. This might be a problem for UE methods like CCS which are designed for balanced training sets. We benchmark existing approaches on two tasks (labeling math solutions as true or false, and detecting malicious bash scripts) using training sets with varying degrees of class imbalance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impossible tasks.</span> Some questions may be too difficult or ambiguous for the elicited LLM to answer decisively, such as fuzzy conceptual research questions that the model does not have enough knowledge to answer. Ideally, elicitation methods should assign confidence scores to predictions that help distinguish tasks the model can answer from those it cannot. We test whether existing UE and E2H approaches do this by applying them to a dataset consisting of both objective math problems and normative political statements, and measuring whether they achieve strong performance on math problems with high confidence scores while assigning lower confidence scores to the normative political statements.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We find that all of the existing UE and E2H approaches we evaluated perform significantly worse on our datasets than on datasets without these challenges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We also study two hopes for improving on previous methods:</p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.1em;">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Combination of UE and E2H.</span> UE methods find concepts in a dataset which are salient to a model, but these might not all be relevant to the task of interest. E2H methods steer a model on a specific easier task, but this might not generalize to the harder task of interest. It may be possible to combine the strengths of both methods, using UE to find salient concepts in the task dataset, and E2H to exclude spurious concepts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ensembling.</span> Even if the most salient concept that UE finds does not correspond to the desired concept, it is plausible that if we use UE to find multiple salient concepts, one of them will correspond to the desired one. Ensembling different predictors obtained in an unsupervised way (described in <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS3" title="3.3.3 Probing methods relying on ensembling or combining UE and E2H ‣ 3.3 Methods ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.3</span></a>) might allow us to leverage this.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Though we find that these sometimes perform better than other approaches at overcoming these challenges, they do not address them reliably. We hope our findings and datasets will guide future work toward developing UE and E2H methods that are robust to the challenges of real-world deployment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Steering LLMs on tasks beyond human capabilities.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs post-trained with erroneous human supervision via SFT can degrade performance relative to base models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and RLHF can lead to reward hacking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skalse et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baker et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and in particular LLMs which are more convincing to humans without being more accurate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Easy-to-hard generalization.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When supervision is of poor quality on the target dataset, one can hope for generalization from supervision on an easy dataset where high-quality supervision is available <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hase et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Supervised truth probes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azaria and Mitchell, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark, 2024</a>)</cite> have also been shown to generalize from simple declarative statements to harder multiple-choice question answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Unsupervised methods.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">UE can also be used to train linear truth probes: the CCS algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al., 2022</a>)</cite> uses a loss function that is minimized when internal activations from logically contradictory statements are given confident, opposite predictions. More recently, it was shown that fine-tuning on labels generated by the ICM algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> (which aims to maximize the logical consistency and mutual predictability of labels according to an LLM’s next-token logprobs) is able to match SFT on golden labels for small training sets. Though previous work demonstrates high performance of these UE and E2H methods on standard benchmarks, we benchmark a variety of these methods on datasets we believe are more representative of challenges we are likely to face when steering models on tasks which are beyond human supervision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Salient non-truth features.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CCS was found to outperform zero-shot performance on ten diverse NLP datasets. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al. (2023)</a></cite> showed salient non-truth features (such as agreement with a character’s preference given in the prompt, or the presence of extraneous tokens) can easily be preferred by CCS over correctness. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laurito et al. (2024)</a></cite> proposed clustering contrastive pairs of activations and normalizing these separately to mitigate the effect of such features. We study this challenge of salient non-truth features in more realistic datasets as well as other challenges and other hopes for overcoming them using not only unsupervised techniques but also E2H ones.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Imbalanced training sets and impossible tasks.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous work showed that issues with imbalanced training sets can be counteracted in a supervised setting with techniques like oversampling, upsampling, class weight adjustment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alnabhan and Branco, 2024</a>)</cite>, or by generating adversarial out-of-distribution examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and that hallucinations on tasks LLMs are not capable enough to answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> can be partially mitigated with supervised learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. To our knowledge there has been no work on steering LLMs on tasks with class imbalance or with impossible tasks in unsupervised settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ensembling predictors and semi-supervised learning.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The two hopes we study are inspired by existing work. Ensembling has been studied in the context of reducing overfitting to spurious features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dietterich, 2000</a>)</cite> and improving generalization of truth probes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>—which we extend by using it in the context of UE and E2H. Mixing UE and E2H is similar to semi-supervised learning, which involves training models with a supervised objective on a small set of labeled data and an unsupervised objective on a large set of unlabeled data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">van Engelen and Hoos, 2020</a>)</cite>. Our hybrid methods are conceptually similar, but the supervised and unsupervised datasets differ by difficulty rather than size—and are probing-based to enable head-to-head comparisons with unsupervised probing methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem statement</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work we compare different approaches to the challenge of poor human supervision. To simulate this scenario for a given task, we assume we have access to:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.1em;">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Examples for the given task without labels (the hard set)—techniques which only use those are UE techniques.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Examples for a significantly easier task with labels (the easy set)—techniques which only use those are E2H techniques.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A pre-trained LLM with no post-training.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During the training phase of each method we cannot use any information about what salient non-truth features are present in the training set, the distribution of ground truth labels in the training set, or whether each example in the training set has an objective truth value. However, we also evaluate ceilings which do have access to this information to gauge the difference in performance due to no supervision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the performance of UE and E2H techniques, we use the ground truth labels of a holdout test set, although this information would not be available for real tasks that are beyond human capability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct datasets for each of the three challenges described in <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Each dataset is derived from an existing dataset and modified to introduce salient non-truth features, class imbalance, or examples that LLMs cannot answer decisively, in a way that is analogous to real situations where humans can’t provide a good supervision signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we discuss the motivation behind the datasets for each challenge and describe how each individual dataset is structured. GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite> is used for multiple challenges to create simple settings that are easily comparable with each other. We use candidate LLM solutions to GSM8K problems provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al. (2025a)</a></cite>. Prior to any modifications described below, we filtered this dataset so that each unique question has one correct and one incorrect solution. Prompt formats used for each dataset are given in <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Prompt formats ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Salient Non-Truth Features</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use datasets with non-truth features which are known to sometimes cause issues with LLMs—namely <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sycophancy</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malmqvist, 2025</a>)</cite>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">political bias</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fulay et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jakob et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">spurious correlations</span> on toxicity classification tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sap et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rosenblatt et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, as well as more superficial features related to text formatting. These issues are normally mitigated using some degree of supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Culotta, 2020a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Culotta, 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chew et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, making them a useful case study for steering models without supervision—where the prompt is the only element that LLMs can rely on to avoid the spurious correlation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GSM8K (added salient features).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each math question in GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, two of the solutions sampled from LLMs are provided, one correct and one incorrect. The task is to identify the correct solution, even when one of the two solutions (chosen at random) has been modified to exhibit a salient feature. The features used here are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.1em;">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sycophancy:</span> The prompt indicates that a character is in agreement with the chosen solution, similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Punctuation:</span> The chosen solution has additional exclamation points, question marks, and periods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tense:</span> The chosen solution is written in past or future tense instead of present tense.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Only one of the above features is added to the dataset during a given experiment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LIAR.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each statement (a comment taken from the PolitiFact website) has a factual correctness label and an uncorrelated political stance label <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang, 2017</a>)</cite>. The task is to classify each statement according to factual correctness or political stance depending on the prompt format.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Civil Comments.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each comment taken from a news site <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Borkan et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, a statement is constructed which asserts that the comment exhibits a specific (target) feature related to civility (e.g. toxicity). The task is to classify each statement as true or false, even when a different (competing) civility-related feature (e.g. sexual explicitness) is present in the dataset. This is repeated for each combination of target and competing feature, to see whether UE and E2H methods consistently favor one feature regardless of its correlation with the truth.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20400v1/salient_gsm8k.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/266;" width="476" height="266"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Performance degrades for the most salient spurious features (left = more salient).<span id="S3.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We show performance of each method’s predictions on unmodified GSM8K vs. their performance on GSM8K when (a) sycophancy, (b) punctuation, and (c) tense features have been added to the dataset with no correlation to the correctness of each solution.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Imbalanced Training Sets</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many safety-relevant applications of LLMs involve detecting rare failures or threats among mostly correct or safe examples. We take datasets from two domains where we expect this could happen (coding and math) and vary the training set distribution to see the effect on UE methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20400v1/salient_liar_civilcomments.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/696;" width="476" height="696"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Most methods discover the most salient feature rather than the feature indicated by the prompt.<span id="S3.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We show performance of each method on classification tasks where there are 2 possible predictions to make, one of which is the desired one, specified by the prompt, and the other one is a spurious one that should be avoided. (a) and (b) are on the LIAR dataset, while (c) and (d) are on the Civil Comments dataset. Political leaning and toxicity are most salient, which is why performance on (a) and (c) is high while performance on (b) and (d) is low.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ctrl-Z.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each example is a sequence of bash commands that is either safe or malicious, taken from data provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite> (see <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Curation of Ctrl-Z ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for how we curated examples from their data). The proportion of safe examples in the training set is set at either 50%, 99%, or 100%—representing a balanced dataset, a dataset with some malicious examples, and a dataset with zero malicious examples, respectively. For each training set distribution, the task is to assign truth scores that rank safe sequences above malicious ones in the test set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GSM8K (imbalanced).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proportion of correct LLM solutions provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al. (2025a)</a></cite> to GSM8K problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite> in the training set is varied between 0% and 100%. For each training set distribution, the task is to assign truth scores that rank correct solutions above incorrect ones in the test set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20400v1/imbalanced_gsm8k.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/259;" width="476" height="259"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of UE and E2H methods on GSM8K for varying proportions of correct claims in the training set. Performance for methods which do not use the training set (zero-shot, random probe, E2H) is plotted with horizontal dashed lines.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Impossible Tasks</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ideally, LLMs should give confident responses to questions within their capability while simultaneously giving low-confidence responses to questions which are not. We construct a dataset to test the ability of UE and E2H methods to elicit each type of response appropriately.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GSM8K + Political Ideologies.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each example is either a mathematical solution to a GSM8K problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite> provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, or a normative claim taken from Political Ideologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nayak, 2024</a>)</cite>, which comprises political statements aligning with either typically liberal or conservative beliefs (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Modifications to Political Ideologies ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for details on how we modified this dataset for our experiments). We would like methods to assign very high truth scores to correct GSM8K solutions, very low truth scores to incorrect GSM8K solutions, and intermediate truth scores to normative political claims.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Methods</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All the methods we benchmark use an LLM to determine if a statement is true or false (without a Chain-of-Thought). We use Llama 3.1 8B for salient non-truth feature experiments and imbalanced GSM8K experiments, and Llama 3.1 70B for impossible task experiments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. For imbalanced Ctrl-Z experiments, we compare both models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We study existing prompting methods, existing linear probe methods, and new probing methods based on our two hopes (ensembling and combining UE and E2H approaches).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Prompting methods</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For data point <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a zero-shot prompt <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which ends with a statement/question (e.g. "Is the above sequence of commands suspicious?") to which a positive or negative token can be appended (e.g. <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Yes</span> or <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">No</span>). Prompting methods take a zero-shot or few-shot prompt, use an LLM to infer next-token logprobs of positive and negative tokens, and assign a truth score equal to the difference of these.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here are the prompting methods we study:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Zero-shot (baseline).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Use the base prompt <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Random few-shot (baseline).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Use a few-shot prompt with randomly assigned (binary) labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bootstrapped few-shot.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Use a few-shot prompt with labels taken from the last of multiple iterations of few-shot prompting on the training set. At each iteration, the model’s predictions from the previous iteration are used in few-shot prompts to label new examples. Compared to few-shot prompting with ICM-generated labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, this method was found to create labels with similar accuracy while being much less expensive to run <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shrivastava et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Golden few-shot (ceiling).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Use a few-shot prompt with ground-truth labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. When some claims are normative, we use Golden few-shot with a mixed objectivity: we use a few-shot prompt with ground-truth labels for objective claims and <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">indeterminate</span> for normative claims.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Previous linear probe methods</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For linear probe methods, each prompt <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is converted into a contrastive pair of prompts <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{+}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{+}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{-}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{-}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by appending the positive token (e.g. “Is [claim] true? Yes”) or negative token (e.g. “Is [claim] true? No”) to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The last-token activations from a specific layer of the LLM are taken for each of these, giving a contrastive pair of activations <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{+}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{+}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{-}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{-}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (we use the layer which gives optimal CCS performance for the unmodified GSM8K dataset). As in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we normalize activations by subtracting the mean activation of each group:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\phi}_{i}^{+}=\phi^{+}_{i}-\mu^{+},\quad\hat{\phi}_{i}^{-}=\phi^{-}_{i}-\mu^{-}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\phi}_{i}^{+}=\phi^{+}_{i}-\mu^{+},\quad\hat{\phi}_{i}^{-}=\phi^{-}_{i}-\mu^{-}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are the averages of all positive-token and negative-token activations in the training set. The aim is for most of the difference between <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{+}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{+}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{-}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{-}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> after normalization to be due to the overall correctness of the claim—prior to normalization, most of the difference is likely due to which token (positive or negative) was appended.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The trained probe assigns a truth score equal to the difference between positive and negative activations projected onto the probe direction. For completely unsupervised methods, we also need to determine whether a positive or a negative score corresponds to a true statement. In these cases, similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we choose the direction which maximizes AUROC.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              We also do this for supervised probes trained on maximally imbalanced datasets (i.e. all examples are from one class only) for comparison with random probes.</span></span></span> Here are the existing probing methods we study:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Random probe (baseline).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Initialize by sampling weights uniformly at random from the unit sphere <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mallen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">CCS.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Train the probe by minimizing the CCS loss on a training set, as described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al. (2022)</a></cite>. The CCS loss is an unsupervised loss function with a term for consistency (lowest when <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are equal and opposite in the probe direction) and confidence (lowest when either <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are very negative in the probe direction).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">PCA.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Perform PCA on differences between contrastive activations <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}^{+}-\phi_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}^{+}-\phi_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> and use the first principal component value as the probe direction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mallen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">E2H.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Train the probe on a labeled easy dataset (the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">larger_than</span> dataset from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark (2024)</a></cite> using supervised cross-entropy loss—we also tried using this combined with more datasets from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark (2024)</a></cite> but preliminary experiments on GSM8K (added salient features) showed similar performance to just using <span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">larger_than</span>. The hope is that it generalizes from the easy to the hard dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Supervised probe (ceiling).</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Train the probe with supervised cross-entropy loss on the training set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark, 2024</a>)</cite>. When some claims are normative, we use supervised probes with a mixed objectivity: we train the probe with supervised cross-entropy loss but give normative claims a target output of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.3 </span>Probing methods relying on ensembling or combining UE and E2H</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study probing methods using the two hopes described in <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">UE + E2H </h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Combination hope). Train the probe using a weighted sum of CCS loss on the training set and supervised loss on an easy dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ensemble methods.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Take a weighted sum of scores from either <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> random probes or the first <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> PCA components (we used <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=128</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=256</annotation></semantics></math>). Weights are determined via either:</p>
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Consensus-weighting</span> (Ensembling hope): for <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, use one of the probes to label the train set. Assign it weight <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if it agrees with the current ensemble for more than 50% of examples or <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise (in order to prevent all probes canceling out), and add it to the ensemble.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E2H-weighting</span> (Combination hope + Ensembling hope): calculate the AUROC of each probe on the easy dataset minus <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>. Assign weights equal to the softmax of these values with temperature <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We measure the performance of each type of probe with each type of weighting method. We found that scaling the probes so that they all gave scores of equal variance on the training set did not give an improvement (see <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Results for Other Methods ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Other methods</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We experimented with variations of the ensembling methods above and with the clustering normalization method proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laurito et al. (2024)</a></cite> but did not run them over the full set of stress-testing datasets. We present results in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Results for Other Methods ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. Overall we do not believe these results meaningfully change our conclusions about the difficulties of overcoming the challenges we present here.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Salient Non-Truth Features</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The accuracy of all methods degrades in the presence of highly salient features.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In Civil Comments. ‣ 3.2.1 Salient Non-Truth Features ‣ 3.2 Datasets ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, when the sycophancy feature is injected into GSM8K, the accuracies of most methods drop relative to the unmodified dataset by over 10 percentage points. Whereas when the punctuation feature is injected into GSM8K, the accuracies of most methods are within 5 percentage points of their accuracies on the unmodified dataset, and when the tense feature is injected, most are within 2 percentage points. We hypothesize that this is because sycophancy is a more salient feature to the LLM than punctuation or grammatical tense, making it more likely to be discovered by unsupervised methods over truthfulness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Most methods label based on feature salience regardless of which feature the prompt asks for.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.2.2 Imbalanced Training Sets ‣ 3.2 Datasets ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a), we show that for the LIAR dataset, whenever the prompt format asks for a political stance label, all methods make predictions which agree more with the ground truth labels for political stance than factual correctness, as desired. However, as shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.2.2 Imbalanced Training Sets ‣ 3.2 Datasets ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b), when the prompt format instead asks for factual correctness labels, all methods aside from ensembles of PCA probes still make predictions more aligned with political leaning labels than factual correctness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.2.2 Imbalanced Training Sets ‣ 3.2 Datasets ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c) and <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.2.2 Imbalanced Training Sets ‣ 3.2 Datasets ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(d) we can see that for every UE and E2H method applied to Civil Comments aside from bootstrapped prompting, the generated labels align with ground truth toxicity labels approximately as much or more than sexual explicitness labels, even when the prompt asks for sexual explicitness labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From these results we hypothesize that political leaning and toxicity features are more salient than factual correctness and sexual explicitness, respectively, and that UE and E2H methods predict labels primarily based on feature salience.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20400v1/imbalanced_ctrl_z.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/869;" width="476" height="869"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of UE and E2H methods on Ctrl-Z for varying proportions of safe bash command sequences in the training set. Performance for methods which do not use the training set (zero-shot, random probe, E2H) are plotted with horizontal dashed lines.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Imbalanced Training Sets</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">UE methods can perform surprisingly well on imbalanced training sets.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S3.F4" title="In GSM8K (imbalanced). ‣ 3.2.2 Imbalanced Training Sets ‣ 3.2 Datasets ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, when the GSM8K training set contains only incorrect mathematical solutions, CCS and PCA probes perform similarly or better than when it is balanced. This is surprising given that, when all training set claims are incorrect, removing information from activations about the final token in the prompt through normalization (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.3.2 Previous linear probe methods ‣ 3.3 Methods ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) should intuitively also remove information about truthfulness (since all incorrect claims appended with a positive token are false and all those with a negative token are true). When all training set claims are correct, CCS and PCA probes perform significantly worse than when the training set is balanced, as expected.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Most methods generalize poorly from safe to malicious bash commands.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.F5" title="In Most methods label based on feature salience regardless of which feature the prompt asks for. ‣ 4.1 Salient Non-Truth Features ‣ 4 Results ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we show the results for performance on distinguishing safe sequences of Ctrl-Z bash commands from malicious ones, for training sets comprising 50%, 99%, or 100% safe examples. No method exceeds 0.75 AUROC when the dataset contains 99% or 100% safe examples except for E2H, which is not impacted by the distribution of the training set (since it only uses a separate easy set) and achieves an AUROC of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.87" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.87</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.87</annotation></semantics></math> when the LLM is Llama 3.1 70B. However, when the LLM is Llama 3.1 8B, this too performs poorly, with an AUROC of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.67</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.67</annotation></semantics></math> despite an AUROC of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75</annotation></semantics></math> being possible when the dataset is balanced (e.g. using CCS).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Impossible tasks</h3>

<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20400v1/impossible_gsm8k.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/361;" width="476" height="361"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of UE and E2H methods on scoring statements from GSM8K + Political Ideologies, measured in terms of AUROC of GSM8K scores (higher the better) and the relative contribution from GSM8K scores to the total variance of the combined dataset (higher the better).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">UE and E2H methods are over-confident on impossible tasks.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a set of truth scores predicted on the combined GSM8K + Political Ideologies dataset, we measure performance by both its AUROC on GSM8K and relative confidence on GSM8K. The latter metric is defined as the contribution from GSM8K to the variance of an equal-weight mixture of scores on GSM8K and Political Ideologies (full definition in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Definition of Relative Confidence Measure ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>). As shown in <a href="#S4.F6" title="In 4.3 Impossible tasks ‣ 4 Results ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, despite some methods achieving an AUROC of over <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math> on GSM8K, all UE or E2H methods achieve a relative confidence of less than 60%, indicating that confidence on objective GSM8K claims is comparable to confidence on normative Political Ideologies claims. Example truth score distributions illustrating good performance and three failure modes on this task are given in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Example truth score distributions for GSM8K + Political Ideologies ‣ Three Concrete Challenges and Two Hopes for the Safety of Unsupervised Elicitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis covers a representative but not exhaustive range of UE and E2H methods. For example, we did not benchmark any methods which use SFT or any weak-to-strong generalization methods (which are based on confidence losses similar to unsupervised techniques studied here). Some of our datasets are not very realistic (for example, mixing GSM8K questions with normative political claims—the large, unrealistic distribution shift between both types of examples caused some methods to differentiate examples based on dataset of origin), though most of them are a significant realism improvement over those used by prior work stress-testing unsupervised methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. There may also be additional mitigation strategies that could improve UE and E2H performance—for example, instead of naively training on a dataset assuming it is balanced, one could use a weak classifier (e.g. zero-shot prompting) to get rough training set labels and resample based on those (however this rebalancing approach could itself run into problems due to the other 2 challenges).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other challenges and hopes.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work focuses on the three challenges that we think pose serious threats to unsupervised elicitation and two hopes that looked most promising, but we do not claim these are the only challenges or only hopes. For example, our work does not tackle issues with scheming AIs that might intentionally interfere with elicitation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlsmith, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and it does not study other hopes like more sophisticated consistency properties than the ones used by CCS and bootstrapping.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The threat of silent unsupervised elicitation failures.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluation of unsupervised elicitation methods is tricky, since by definition unsupervised elicitation methods are most useful when in-distribution ground truth labels are not available. Using only benchmarks like GSM8K or standard preference modeling datasets to validate methods would likely produce overoptimistic results if real deployments have challenges like the ones studied here. Thus, for applications like using UE to detect AIs breaking the law in ways humans would not notice (where in-distribution evaluations are not possible), the inadequacy of an unsupervised method may remain silent until the method fails catastrophically.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Recommendations for future work.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to mitigate the dangers of silent unsupervised elicitation failures, work into building better unsupervised elicitation methods should use evaluation datasets that capture challenges of the most important UE applications, for example by using the datasets presented in our work, or by using new datasets targeted at the main challenges UE methods are supposed to overcome.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced three realistic challenges for UE and E2H approaches to training models on tasks beyond human capabilities (salient non-truth features, training set imbalance, and impossible tasks) and constructed datasets that exhibit each. The prompting and probing methods studied all suffer from the presence of highly salient non-truth features, sometimes suffer from imbalanced datasets, and are often overconfident on normative questions. Our ensembling variants improve robustness to some non-truth features but do not fully mitigate the issue. These results suggest that previous evaluations of UE and E2H methods may have yielded overoptimistic results, since they were done on idealized datasets. We hope the challenges and benchmarks presented here help guide the development of elicitation methods that are reliable when human supervision is unavailable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents challenges for the safety of unsupervised elicitation and easy-to-hard generalization methods. We hope that by showing how these challenges can significantly degrade the performance of different methods, we instill caution in the development of new methods; strong performance on benchmarks might not transfer to real-world applications. This also applies to our benchmarks. Though we hope that awareness of these challenges will contribute to making elicitation methods more robust, even if a method performs well on datasets with the challenges introduced here, real tasks on which elicitation methods might be used could have other unforeseen challenges, and the lack of any ground truth labels in a real deployment scenario means failures could happen silently.</p>
</div>
</section>
